/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An aircraft has six degrees of freedom: three translations (fore-aft, lateral, vertical) and three rotations.
   - The ailerons also cause adverse yaw: the down-going aileron makes more drag and pulls the nose away from the turn.
   - A pilot commands a deflection, not a force.
   - Some syllabi group these as auxiliary controls.
   - The Cessna 172R, a four-seat single-engine trainer, has fully mechanical controls: a control wheel (yoke) on a column, two rudder pedals, a throttle, a mixture control and a manual elevator trim wheel.
   - Rods and cables do the same job by different means; the chapter on push-pull rods and cables compares them.
   - The chain is sized by its gearing G: the stick travel per unit of surface rotation.
   - F is the stick force in N; H is the aerodynamic hinge moment in N·m; G is the gearing in metres of stick travel per radian of surface rotation (multiply mm per degree by 57.3 and divide by 1000).
   - Answer.
*/
