/* book exam traps: Putting roll on the rudder. The rudder yaws the aircraft; the ailerons roll it, and they also cause adverse yaw. | Saying large gearing makes the controls heavy. F = H ÷ G: many millimetres of stick per degree give light forces and a sluggish response; few millimetres per degree give heavy forces and a twitchy one. | Mixing the axes: roll is about the longitudinal axis, pitch about the lateral axis, yaw about the vertical axis. | Using millimetres in a hinge-moment sum. Convert the horn arm to metres (80 mm = 0.080 m), so the answer is in N·m, not N·mm. | Thinking ailerons turn the aircraft directly. They bank it; the horizontal component of lift does the turning. */
