/* book: lead: A pilot's command must travel from the stick or pedal to a surface far away. Two mechanical ways do this. A push-pull rod is a tube that carries the command in both directions, push and pull, and needs bell cranks and guides to route it. A cable system carries only pull, so it always runs as a matched pair: one cable pulls the surface one way while the other slackens. Rods suit short, straight run */
/* book Figure 1.2.1: The worked example’s 3 m run both ways. The cable carries only pull, so a return cable completes the loop; 1.5 kN stretches it 3.75 mm. The rod carries push and pull, but over 3 m its Euler load is only 207 N; three guides cut it into four 750 mm bays and raise that to 3317 N. */
