/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A push-pull rod is a thin metal tube with an end fitting at each end.
   - Pcr is the critical buckling load in N; E is Young's modulus in Pa; I is the second moment of area of the tube section in m⁴; L is the unsupported length in m; K is the end-condition factor, 1 for pin-ended rods.
   - σ is axial stress in Pa; F is axial force in N; A is cross-section area in m².
   - A bell crank is a two-armed lever on a pivot.
   - Aircraft control cable is stranded steel: many thin wires twisted into strands, strands into a cable.
   - Pulleys redirect the cable.
   - A flexible push-pull cable is different: a stranded inner core slides inside a flexible conduit.
   - ΔL is cable stretch in m; F is tension in N; L is cable length in m; A is the metal cross-section area in m²; E is Young's modulus in Pa.
   - Backlash, also called lost motion, is the small movement at a joint before the load is carried.
   - Friction rises with every pulley and fairlead, so a long cable run can feel heavy.
*/
