/* book exam traps: Drawing one cable that both pushes and pulls the surface. A cable can only pull, so the surface needs a second cable pulling the other way. | Checking a push-pull rod in compression by stress alone. A long thin rod fails by buckling at a load far below its stress limit. | Forgetting pre-tension in the return cable. A slack cable gives lost motion and a delayed response when the control reverses. | Confusing a bell crank with an idler lever. A bell crank redirects and can change the motion ratio; an idler only supports and redirects. | Mixing mm² and m² in ΔL = FL/(AE). Using 6 instead of 6 × 10⁻⁶ gives a stretch a million times too large. */
